Yes, $\chi_2(D)u$ also satisfies the Schrödinger equation. Here is the proof.

## Proof

Work in one spatial dimension (the multiplier $\chi_{[2,4]}(\xi)$ is an interval). Use the Fourier convention
$$\hat f(\xi)=\mathcal F f(\xi)=\int_{\mathbb R} f(x)e^{-ix\xi}\,dx,\qquad \mathcal F^{-1}g(x)=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{\mathbb R} g(\xi)e^{ix\xi}\,d\xi,$$
so that $\mathcal F(\partial_x^2 f)(\xi)=-\xi^2\hat f(\xi)$.

**Step 1. Fourier form of the equation for $u$.**  
Taking the Fourier transform of $i\partial_t u+\Delta u=0$ in $x$ gives
$$i\,\partial_t\hat u(\xi,t)-\xi^2\hat u(\xi,t)=0,$$
hence $\partial_t\hat u(\xi,t)=-i\xi^2\hat u(\xi,t)$, and therefore
$$\hat u(\xi,t)=e^{-i\xi^2 t}\,\widehat\phi(\xi). \tag{1}$$

**Step 2. Fourier form of $\chi_2(D)u$.**  
Set $v(x,t):=\chi_2(D)u(x,t)=\mathcal F^{-1}\!\bigl(\chi_{[2,4]}(\xi)\hat u(\xi,t)\bigr)(x)$. By definition of the inverse transform,
$$\hat v(\xi,t)=\chi_{[2,4]}(\xi)\,\hat u(\xi,t). \tag{2}$$

**Step 3. $v$ satisfies the Schrödinger equation.**  
Since $\chi_{[2,4]}(\xi)$ is independent of $t$, differentiating (2) in $t$ and using the equation for $\hat u$,
$$\partial_t\hat v(\xi,t)=\chi_{[2,4]}(\xi)\,\partial_t\hat u(\xi,t)=\chi_{[2,4]}(\xi)\bigl(-i\xi^2\hat u(\xi,t)\bigr)=-i\xi^2\hat v(\xi,t).$$
Multiplying by $i$,
$$i\,\partial_t\hat v(\xi,t)=\xi^2\hat v(\xi,t),$$
i.e.
$$i\,\partial_t\hat v(\xi,t)-\xi^2\hat v(\xi,t)=0.$$
Taking the inverse Fourier transform (and using $\mathcal F(\Delta v)=-\xi^2\hat v$) yields
$$i\,\partial_t v(x,t)+\Delta v(x,t)=0.$$

**Step 4. Initial data.**  
At $t=0$, by (2),
$$\hat v(\xi,0)=\chi_{[2,4]}(\xi)\widehat\phi(\xi),$$
so $v(x,0)=\chi_2(D)\phi(x)$.

**Conclusion.** The operator $\chi_2(D)$ is a Fourier multiplier with a symbol $\chi_{[2,4]}(\xi)$ that is bounded and independent of $t$. In Fourier variables both $\partial_t$ and $\Delta$ act by multiplication (by $1$ and by $-\xi^2$ respectively), so the multiplier $\chi_{[2,4]}(\xi)$ commutes with the Schrödinger operator $i\partial_t+\Delta$, equivalently with the propagator $e^{it\Delta}$. Thus
$$\chi_2(D)u(x,t)=\chi_2(D)e^{it\Delta}\phi(x)=e^{it\Delta}\bigl(\chi_2(D)\phi\bigr)(x),$$
which is precisely the solution of the Schrödinger equation with initial data $\chi_2(D)\phi$. Hence $\chi_2(D)u$ satisfies the Schrödinger equation.

